---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import TerminalBlock from "../../components/TerminalBlock";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Convention Enforcement - AI Training Guide">
  <PageShell chapterId="practical-exercise" subChapterId="convention-enforcement">
    <div class="prose space-y-6">

      <p>
        AI coding assistants are capable of producing working code, but "working" and "consistent" are not the same thing. Left to its own devices, the AI will make reasonable but arbitrary choices about naming, file structure, component patterns, and state management. Those choices may differ from what your project already does, and inconsistency adds friction for every developer who touches the code next.
      </p>

      <p>
        This exercise demonstrates how a single file reference in your prompt can dramatically improve consistency, and what happens when you leave it out.
      </p>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-4">The Golden Master Pattern</h2>
        <p>
          A <strong>golden master</strong> is an existing, well-implemented feature that serves as a reference for how new features should be built. Instead of describing every convention in your prompt, you point the AI at a real file and say "do it like this." The AI picks up on naming patterns, folder structure, state management approach, component composition, and styling conventions without you having to enumerate them.
        </p>
        <p>
          You've already seen this idea in the <a href="/task-breakdowns/including-context" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800 underline">Including Context</a> chapter. Now you'll experience the difference firsthand.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-4">Exercise 1: Without a Reference</h2>
        <p>
          Pick a feature from your task breakdowns that you haven't implemented yet. Open a <strong>fresh chat session</strong> and give the AI just the task breakdown, with no reference to existing code.
        </p>

        <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
          <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">1</div>
          <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Prompt Without a Golden Master</h3>
          <TerminalBlock
            title="Your Prompt"
            lines={[
              { type: "command", command: "Implement Task 1 from @docs/tasks/pomodoro-timer.md" },
            ]}
            client:visible
          />
          <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mt-4">
            Let the AI complete the task. Don't intervene or provide additional guidance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
          <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">2</div>
          <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Examine the Output</h3>
          <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
            Before reverting, take note of the choices the AI made. Compare them against your existing features:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-stone-600">
            <li><strong>File placement:</strong> Did it put files in the same directories your other features use?</li>
            <li><strong>Naming:</strong> Do component names, hook names, and type names follow the same conventions as your existing code?</li>
            <li><strong>State management:</strong> Did it use the same storage hooks and patterns as your other features?</li>
            <li><strong>Component structure:</strong> Does the component composition match how your existing dashboard cards are built?</li>
            <li><strong>Styling:</strong> Are Tailwind classes used in the same way (spacing scale, color palette, responsive patterns)?</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
          <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">3</div>
          <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Revert</h3>
          <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
            Discard the changes so you can try again with a reference.
          </p>
          <TerminalBlock
            title="Revert Changes"
            lines={[
              { type: "command", command: "git checkout ." },
            ]}
            client:visible
          />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-4">Exercise 2: With a Golden Master</h2>
        <p>
          Now open a <strong>fresh chat session</strong> and try the same task, but this time reference an existing feature as a golden master.
        </p>

        <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
          <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">1</div>
          <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Prompt With a Golden Master</h3>
          <TerminalBlock
            title="Your Prompt"
            lines={[
              { type: "command", command: "Implement Task 1 from @docs/tasks/pomodoro-timer.md. Follow the same patterns, file structure, and conventions used in @src/components/HabitTracker.tsx." },
            ]}
            client:visible
          />
          <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mt-4">
            The file reference gives the AI a concrete example to follow. It will mirror the naming scheme, the component structure, the hook patterns, and the styling approach from that file.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
          <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">2</div>
          <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Compare the Results</h3>
          <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
            Look at the same areas you checked before. The difference in consistency should be noticeable:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-stone-600">
            <li>Files placed in the expected directories</li>
            <li>Naming that matches your existing conventions</li>
            <li>The same state management patterns reused</li>
            <li>Component composition that feels like it belongs in the same project</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <Callout variant="tip" title="Why Not Just Rely on AGENTS.md?">
          <p>
            Your AGENTS.md file describes conventions at a high level, and the AI does read it. But a golden master shows conventions in practice, with all the small details that are hard to articulate in prose. Think of AGENTS.md as the rules and a golden master as the worked example. Both help, but the example is more precise.
          </p>
        </Callout>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-stone-800 mt-8 mb-4">When to Use a Golden Master</h2>
        <p>
          You don't need a golden master for every prompt. It's most valuable when:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-stone-600">
          <li><strong>Building a new feature that parallels an existing one</strong> (new dashboard card, new page with similar layout)</li>
          <li><strong>Onboarding to a codebase</strong> where you're still learning the conventions yourself</li>
          <li><strong>Working in a team</strong> where consistency across contributors matters</li>
          <li><strong>The feature involves patterns the AI might not infer</strong> from AGENTS.md alone (custom hooks, specific component hierarchies)</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="mt-4">
          For small, isolated changes (fixing a bug, updating a string), a golden master reference adds noise without much benefit. Use your judgment.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="border-t border-stone-200 pt-8 mt-8">
        <p class="text-stone-800 font-medium">
          If you're satisfied with the output from the golden master approach, commit it and move on to the next exercise. Going forward, consider which existing files make good references when you prompt the AI for new features.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </PageShell>
</MainLayout>
